raw:
{
  "slots": {
    "question": [
      {
        "claim": "Did the Democrats’ 2024 campaign ad labeling Trump a convicted criminal worsen Democrats’ views of people with criminal records generally?",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "this study explores what may have been the impact of propagating such labels on Democrats’ perceptions of\npeople with criminal records",
            "page": 4
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "How did the ad affect Democrats’ feelings of being represented and their judgments about whether elected officials serve the public or themselves?",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "to what extent does the propagation of such stigmatizing terminology impact how well\nDemocrats perceive to be represented by their own elected officials?",
            "page": 4
          },
          {
            "text": "their perceptions of elected officials’ motivations\n(i.e., the extent to which they are motivated by serving the public, as opposed to their own self-interest.)",
            "page": 4
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Can hearing from a formerly incarcerated legislator before seeing the ad counter its potential harms?",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "this study seeks to understand whether\nproviding a counterexample to Trump – highlighting the perspective of a formerly system-involved representative on the\npropagation of this label by the Democratic party – may counter-act potential negative impacts of the Party’s Trump advertisement.",
            "page": 4
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Does hearing the legislator’s perspective first reduce Democrats’ support for using the ad?",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Hypothesis 1A: Exposure to the counter-narrative first, describing a public service-oriented individual who has a criminal record, will significantly decrease support for the use of the advertisement, on average, relative to exposure to\nthe advertisement of Trump being labeled as a “convicted criminal” alone.",
            "page": 6
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "As a secondary question, do the effects differ for people affected by the justice system through their own or a loved one’s involvement?",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "supplemental analyses will also explore potential heterogeneity by\nsystem-impact status (i.e., whether someone identifies as having previously been involved in the system or impacted by\nthe system through their loved one’s involvement.)",
            "page": 7
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "central_claim": [
      {
        "claim": "The experiment finds a trade-off: the Trump ad improved Democrats’ feelings of representation but worsened their views of people with criminal records. Hearing from a formerly incarcerated public servant first almost entirely offset the latter effect.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Results suggest that exposure\nto the Trump advertisement with the “convicted criminal” label had significant, negative impacts on Democrats’ perceptions of individuals with records, but improved\nperceived feelings of representation.",
            "page": 1
          },
          {
            "text": "when paired with (and preceded by) a counter-narrative of a dedicated,\nsystem-impacted state representative who speaks to the harm of the “criminal” label, these effects were almost entirely\nameliorated",
            "page": 15
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The author argues for testing campaign messages that acknowledge Trump’s convictions while elevating the perspectives of formerly system-involved representatives, rather than relying on stigmatizing attacks.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "a viable, alternate route for political messaging\nduring the 2024 presidential election could have been to acknowledge, but juxtapose, the nature of Trump’s convictions and his\nbroadcasted attitude about his convictions with effective counter-narratives.",
            "page": 18
          },
          {
            "text": "utilizing political messaging\nthat elevates dedicated, justice-involved voices within the party rather than rely on\nstigmatizing attacks should be strongly considered and tested.",
            "page": 1
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "evidence": [
      {
        "claim": "The online survey recruited 2,500 fluent English-speaking U.S. Democrats through Prolific on July 12–13, 2025.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "The survey was fielded through Prolific, an online survey platform that is commonly utilized for conducting survey-based\nresearch",
            "page": 8
          },
          {
            "text": "2,500 participants were\nrecruited for this study, between July 12th and 13th of 2025 [60]. This survey was fielded amongst individuals residing within\nthe United States who spoke fluent English and were members of the Democratic Party.",
            "page": 8
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Of 2,729 survey attempts, 2,439 remained for analysis after screening for failed attention checks, duplicates, missing or invalid completion codes, and likely bot responses.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Of the 2,729 total attempts, 2,481 were retained after dropping those who failed the attention check,\nwere duplicate observations based on stored Prolific identifier embedded data, or provided the wrong completion code",
            "page": 8
          },
          {
            "text": "Approximately 1.69% of responses post-removal of duplicates, incorrect/no codes, and failed attention checks were\nflagged as not being a likely human response, and were also dropped. No observations were flagged as coming from the\nsame IP addressing, leaving the remaining, final analytical sample size of 2,439 participants.",
            "page": 8
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The analytic sample was 59.4% women and 70.6% White. Its mean age was 43.4 years, 60.8% had at least a four-year degree, and 21.4% identified as system-impacted.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "a majority of which (59.4%) identified as women, with\n36.8% identifying as men, and 3.8% identifying as non-binary and/or another gender identity. Most participants were White\n(70.6%)",
            "page": 8
          },
          {
            "text": "Most participants had a four-year degree or higher (60.8%) and 21.4% of the sample identified as being system-impacted.\nThe average age of participants was 43.4 years old.",
            "page": 8
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Colorado State University’s institutional review board approved the study, and participants gave informed consent.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "This study was approved by the Colorado State University Institutional Review Board (IRB), Protocol #7059. All participants in this study provided informed consent for participation.",
            "page": 4
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The randomized experiment varied the order of the stimuli and outcome questions. The control group answered the main outcomes before either stimulus (813); the ad-alone group saw the ad before the outcomes and the narrative afterward (814); the narrative-first group saw the narrative, then the ad, then the outcomes (812). Ad-specific questions followed the ad in every arm.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "the 2,439 participants were allocated approximately evenly across one of three different treatment groups: a) the\nControl group (n = 813); b) the Trump Ad Alone group (n = 814); and c) the Counter-Narrative First group (n = 812).",
            "page": 4
          },
          {
            "text": "the Control group, which presented all outcome questions first, followed by the Trump advertisement stimulus (and associated questions related to the advertisement), followed by the counter-narrative.",
            "page": 5
          },
          {
            "text": "The Trump Ad Alone group\npresented participants with the advertisement (and its associated questions) first, followed by the remaining outcome\nquestions, and subsequently the counter-narrative.",
            "page": 5
          },
          {
            "text": "Finally, the Counter-Narrative First group presented the Trump advertisement (and its associated questions) only after being shown the counter-narrative. Then the outcomes were presented.",
            "page": 5
          },
          {
            "text": "Participants were immediately presented three questions about the advertisement on\nthe same page",
            "page": 5
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Every arm saw the same ad stimulus: a description of its campaign use and Trump’s mugshot overlaid with the words CONVICTED CRIMINAL.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Regardless of when participants were presented with the Trump advertisement, the content was (importantly) constant\nacross treatment arms.",
            "page": 5
          },
          {
            "text": "Participants were presented with the advertisement, including a description of its use by the Democratic party in 2024 presidential election.",
            "page": 5
          },
          {
            "text": "President Donald J. Trump’s mugshot, which\nwas labeled, in all capital letters, “CONVICTED CRIMINAL.”",
            "page": 5
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The counter-narrative was a lightly edited, text-only excerpt from Michael Lyle’s Nevada Current article. It described Jovan Jackson’s advocacy after prison and his objection to equating people who sought rehabilitation with Trump.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "The counter-narrative, on the other hand, provided no visual stimulus, but rather an excerpt for participants to read.\nThis excerpt was from the article entitled, “Could a formerly incarcerated lawmaker reshape justice policy in the NV\nLegislature?,” by Michael Lyle, published in the Nevada Current on November 5, 2024 [55].",
            "page": 5
          },
          {
            "text": "The text was lightly edited for\nbrevity, to remove mention of the state in which this individual is an elected official, and their first name, to more narrowly\ndefine the counter-narrative treatment.",
            "page": 5
          },
          {
            "text": "Every election cycle he knocked on doors for candidates who backed criminal justice reforms, and every legislative session he would lobby for what he believes are smarter policies that affect\npeople impacted by the justice system.",
            "page": 6
          },
          {
            "text": "Jackson found the contrast hurtful and undercut the work he had done since being incarcerated. He said there is a clear difference between a formerly incarcerated person who sought rehabilitation, served\ntheir time and was released, and Trump.",
            "page": 6
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The study averaged and standardized the items in each of its four multi-item outcome indices. The index on people with criminal records used five seven-point agreement items and had a Cronbach’s alpha of 0.71.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "The remaining four outcomes are measured as indices, averaged over each question included in the index, and subsequently standardized for ease of interpretation.",
            "page": 7
          },
          {
            "text": "The perceptions of individuals with criminal records outcome included\nan index, where participants were presented with five statements and were asked how much they agreed with each on\na seven-point Likert scale from “Strongly disagree” to “Strongly agree.”",
            "page": 7
          },
          {
            "text": "the Cronbach’s alpha coefficient was 0.71, reflecting the lower end of the threshold\nfor adequate reliability [59].",
            "page": 7
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The value-of-representation item asked whether a wide range of life experiences among representatives strengthens democracy.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "The perceived value of representation outcome was measured by agreement to\nthe statement, “Having representatives with a wide array of life experiences is crucial to strengthening our democracy,”",
            "page": 7
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The complexity-of-representation item asked whether diversity of life experience is beneficial only if representatives effectively advocate for constituents.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "the complexity of representation question was measured by agreement to the statement, “Having representatives\nwith a wide array of life experiences is really only beneficial when our representatives effectively advocate for the needs of\ntheir constituents.”",
            "page": 7
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Perceived quality of federal Democratic representation was measured with three agreement items, including whether federal Democrats respect and tolerate different kinds of people.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "The perceived quality of Democratic representation in government index included three items, measured on a sevenpoint Likert scale from “Strongly disagree” to “Strongly agree,” including statements such as “Democrats in federal government are respectful and tolerant of different types of people.”",
            "page": 7
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Four items measured perceived public-serving versus self-interested motives, distinguishing Democrats from officials of all parties at both the state and federal levels.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "The motives index included four questions, asking participants the extent to which they felt that\nelected officials were motivated to serve the public, as opposed to their own self-interest.",
            "page": 7
          },
          {
            "text": "The four questions were specific to\nall party members in either state or federal government and Democrats in state or federal government.",
            "page": 7
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The ad-support index combined perceived effectiveness and explicit support for using the ad. A separate exploratory item asked whether respondents thought most Democrats supported it.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "The two items were again measured the same seven-point\nLikert agreement scale and included: a) “This is an effective advertisement;” and b) “I support use of this advertisement.”",
            "page": 7
          },
          {
            "text": "the extent to which participants felt that most\nDemocrats supported the use of the advertisement.",
            "page": 8
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The manipulation check asked participants to report whether they had an emotional reaction to the counter-narrative.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "a manipulation check was included after exposure to the\nnarrative, specifically asking participants to state the extent to which they agreed with the statement, “I had an emotional\nreaction to this narrative.”",
            "page": 8
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The study preregistered two sets of comparisons: narrative before ad versus ad alone, and ad exposure versus no ad exposure before the main outcome questions. The data, code, preregistration, and analysis plan are available on OSF.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "All data and code, as well as the pre-registration and pre-analysis plan for this study are available via OSF.",
            "page": 6
          },
          {
            "text": "the first (Hypothesis Set 1) corresponds with testing the impact of exposure to\nperspective-getting counter-narrative immediately before exposure to the Trump ad, relative to only being presented\nwith the Trump ad.",
            "page": 6
          },
          {
            "text": "The second set (Hypothesis Set 2) includes tests of the advertisement’s impact, relative to no\nexposure to the advertisement prior to answering outcome questions.",
            "page": 6
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Average treatment effects were estimated by least squares with HC-2 robust standard errors, using the ad-alone arm as the reference. The paper reports treatment-only models and models adjusted for prespecified demographic, system-impact, and racial-attitude covariates.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "To estimate each ATE, least squares regressions with heteroskedasticity-consistent standard error\nestimators, HC-2, were utilized. Both the treatment indicator only and the primary models (which include covariates, as\npre-specified), are reported for transparency.",
            "page": 10
          },
          {
            "text": "Each of the models in this analysis utilize the full study sample and set the\nreference category in the regression for the treatment indicator to be those assigned to the Trump Ad Alone group",
            "page": 10
          },
          {
            "text": "a) gender; b) race and/or ethnicity; c) education (less than four year degree indicator); d) whether\nsomeone identifies as being system-impacted or not; e) age; and f) the pre-treatment racial attitudes index.",
            "page": 9
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The author checked covariate balance with least-squares regressions and found no statistically significant imbalance in the two comparisons used for hypothesis testing.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Tests for potential imbalance (i.e.,\nsignificant differences in sociodemographic factors by treatment group assignment) were performed with least squares\nregression, utilizing heteroskedasticity-consistent standard error estimator, HC-2",
            "page": 8
          },
          {
            "text": "There were no statistically significant differences\nbetween either the Control and Trump Ad Alone groups, nor the Trump Ad Alone and Counter-Narrative First group.",
            "page": 9
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Secondary analyses examined individual items in the criminal-record perceptions index, sociodemographic predictors, and whether treatment effects differed by system-impact status. The subgroup analysis tested interactions and presented estimates from separate subgroup models.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "treatment effects",
            "page": 11
          },
          {
            "text": "on individual items for the perceptions of individuals with criminal records index, given its marginally acceptable reliability\ncoefficient; b) summarization of the sociodemographic factors that were significantly predictive of the outcomes of interest;\nand c) heterogeneity in results by system-impact status.",
            "page": 12
          },
          {
            "text": "the significance of the interaction term in the full model (i.e., treatment\nindicator terms and their interactions with the indicator for a participant identifying as being system-impacted)",
            "page": 13
          },
          {
            "text": "conditional average treatment effects (CATEs)\npresented in Fig 3 are from models estimated on each sub-sample.",
            "page": 14
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "key_findings": [
      {
        "claim": "Seeing the Trump ad before answering the outcome questions lowered positive views of people with criminal records by about 0.84 standard deviations compared with answering before seeing the ad.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "exposure to the Trump advertisement alone significantly decreased positive perceptions of individuals with criminal records ( β̂0 = −0.835, SE = 0.044)",
            "page": 11
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Seeing Jackson’s counter-narrative before the ad raised positive views of people with criminal records by about 0.83 standard deviations relative to the ad alone, almost exactly matching the size of the ad’s negative effect.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "exposure to the counter-narrative prior to being presented with the Trump advertisement increased positive perceptions of individuals with criminal records by more than 0.8 standard deviation (SD) units, compared to those\npresented with the Trump advertisement alone ( β̂2 = 0.83, SE = 0.041).",
            "page": 11
          },
          {
            "text": "these effects were almost entirely\nameliorated ( β̂2 = 0.833, whereas β̂0 = −0.835).",
            "page": 15
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The ad improved perceived representation quality but reduced the value respondents placed on diverse life experiences in office. It also increased perceived public-serving motives, although that last result did not survive the Bonferroni correction.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "exposure to the Trump advertisement significantly increased feelings of being well represented by elected officials and the extent to which Democrats perceived\nthat elected officials were motivated by public service over self-interest.",
            "page": 15
          },
          {
            "text": "It is possible that exposure to the Trump advertisement encouraged individuals to agree less in the value\nof diversity of life experiences",
            "page": 15
          },
          {
            "text": "Of the above significant outcomes, only the public-serving motives outcome would not be significant after multiple comparisons corrections, particularly Bonferroni.",
            "page": 10
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Compared with the ad alone, the counter-narrative increased the value placed on diverse life experiences by about 0.79 standard deviations but lowered ratings of federal Democratic representation by about 0.33. It did not significantly change the complexity item; its small negative effect on perceived public-serving motives did not survive correction.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "the initial exposure to the counter-narrative demonstrating a 0.79 SD unit increase in the perceived value of diversity\nof life experience as an important component of strengthening democracy, relative to those only presented with the Trump\nadvertisement before answering outcome questions",
            "page": 11
          },
          {
            "text": "exposure to the counter-narrative significantly decreased quality of Democratic representation in federal government, relative to just Trump advertisement exposure ( β̂2 = −0.332, SE = 0.048).",
            "page": 11
          },
          {
            "text": "no significant differences between the Counter-Narrative First and Trump Ad Alone groups on the outcome related to the\ncomplexity of representation ( β̂2 = −0.062, SE = 0.048).",
            "page": 11
          },
          {
            "text": "reduced the extent to which participants believed that elected officials’ motivations were to serve\nthe public, versus serving their own self-interest ( β̂2 = −0.098, SE = 0.048).",
            "page": 11
          },
          {
            "text": "this finding would no longer be significant after applying a Bonferroni multiple comparisons\ncorrection.",
            "page": 11
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Hearing the counter-narrative first reduced overall support for the ad. When the two items were analyzed separately, explicit support declined significantly, but perceived effectiveness did not.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "exposure to the counter-narrative significantly decreased support for the Trump advertisement, relative to the Trump advertisement alone ( β̂2 = −0.172 SE = 0.048).",
            "page": 11
          },
          {
            "text": "Both coefficients are negative,\nincluding that of the item “This is an effective advertisement” and “I support the use of this advertisement”, though only the\nexplicit support for the advertisement item was statistically significant.",
            "page": 11
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "scope_and_limits": [
      {
        "claim": "The study is preliminary evidence about a campaign messaging strategy, not a demonstration of what the strategy did to the electorate during the campaign. Its sample was limited to Democrats.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "this work should be considered as preliminary evidence of potential effects of the Democratic Party’s messaging strategy, and a line of work that warrants far more empirical attention than it has received.",
            "page": 16
          },
          {
            "text": "the study sample focused only on their constituents.",
            "page": 8
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Without a counter-narrative-only arm, the experiment cannot isolate the effect of Jackson’s account when no Trump ad follows it.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "the counter-narrative arm cannot be directly compared to the pure control arm to test the\nimpact of the counter-narrative alone because it also involves exposure to the Trump advertisement.",
            "page": 16
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Because the mugshot and the label were presented together, the experiment cannot separate the effect of the image from the effect of the wording.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "it is not possible to disentangle the impact of the image from\nthe stigmatizing terminology imposed, and thus, this is recommended for exploration in future work.",
            "page": 17
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The author cannot establish that every online response was human; the prevalence of AI-assisted responding to closed-ended surveys remains unclear.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "the proliferation of generative AI should cause concerns over the humanity of the responses provided within\nthe survey.",
            "page": 17
          },
          {
            "text": "Still, the propensity for the standard online survey taker to utilize AI agents to\ncomplete close-ended surveys is not well understood.",
            "page": 17
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "It is unclear whether the effects would replicate in other contexts or less transparent designs. The manipulation check reduces but does not eliminate concern that participants gave socially desirable answers.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "the extent to which these results would replicate in other contexts and in less-transparent designs is unclear",
            "page": 17
          },
          {
            "text": "it could be the case that participants were subject to the Hawthorne effect, resulting in\noutcomes being driven – in some capacity – by social desirability bias.",
            "page": 17
          },
          {
            "text": "helps alleviate some\nconcerns but does not rule this out.",
            "page": 17
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The one-time survey does not establish how long either message’s effects last.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "for how long do the effects of the counter-narrative\nprime before the advertisement and the advertisement itself persist",
            "page": 17
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The study does not establish whether repeated exposure would produce different effects from a single exposure.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "how may effects vary by single versus repeated\nexposure to the advertisement and/or the counter-narrative prime?",
            "page": 17
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Changes in self-reported attitudes do not show that the messages would change decisions or behavior, including voting.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "to what extent would these treatments translate to shifted decision-making and behavior, as opposed to self-reported perceptions and attitudes?",
            "page": 17
          },
          {
            "text": "for understanding advertisement effects in particular, especially as it relates to potential\nvoting behavior, timing of future (field) experimental studies should be in line with election periods.",
            "page": 17
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The study ran after the election outcome was known, so it does not show whether the same messages would have the same effects during an active campaign.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "this current study was conducted in July of 2025, after the outcome of the election was known and not actively\nduring the campaign. Whether these results replicate during campaign periods is an open question, perhaps especially so\nfor the advertisement’s impacts in particular.",
            "page": 17
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The representation measures do not capture all the ways people may feel represented, such as policy alignment or symbolic and active representation across identities.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "future studies should also aim to include a wider array of questions, both related to perceptions of individuals\nwith criminal records and feelings of being represented.",
            "page": 17
          },
          {
            "text": "in terms of policy position, symbolic and active representation on myriad dimensions of identity, etc.), that may be additionally important to explore",
            "page": 18
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The criminal-record perceptions index was adequately but not strongly reliable, so the author calls for a stronger set of measures.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "given the reliability statistics for the perceptions of individuals with criminal records index\nwas satisfactory but not necessarily strong, utilizing a more robust set of measures is an important next step.",
            "page": 18
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Racial attitudes were measured before treatment and used as covariates, so the study does not show whether the messages changed them.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "racial attitudes were measured as covariates and presented before stimuli",
            "page": 18
          },
          {
            "text": "As such, this should be prioritized as an outcome in future research.",
            "page": 18
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The study does not settle whether the counter-narrative reduced stigma broadly or instead led respondents to see some people with records as more deserving than others.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "While it is possible that the intervention constructs a new category of deservingness, rather than reducing stigma, the\nempirical relationship between attitudes and personal connection suggests that reduction of stigma is more likely [55–57].\nStill, future research should clarify this relationship more deeply.",
            "page": 16
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "theoretical_positioning": [
      {
        "claim": "The paper extends social categorization theory to campaign messaging: treating Trump as a salient member of a category may change how viewers judge other people in that category.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Leaning upon social categorization theory, propagating such\nlabels on Trump may thereby spillover, impacting constituents’ perspectives of individuals with records more broadly.",
            "page": 1
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The paper combines perspective-getting with a counter-stereotypical example and applies that approach to political representation. The legislator’s story is designed both to encourage empathy and to signal a commitment to justice-system reform.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Specifically, this counter-narrative\nutilized perspective-getting, sharing the experience of a state Assemblymember who was previously incarcerated (i.e., has\na criminal record,) and is dedicated to using their platform to serve the public and advocate for system-impacted populations being represented in government.",
            "page": 4
          },
          {
            "text": "well as one that signals their policy alignment (i.e., dedication to justice system reform), would be well-suited to addressing both the affective and policy representation avenues for bolstering support.",
            "page": 4
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The discussion tentatively applies Prospect Theory’s reference-point idea to the representation results: the ad may make other elected officials look better by making Trump the benchmark.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Collectively, these results may\nsimply be Prospect Theory in action: Donald J. Trump may be serving as reference point that may influence Democrats to see other elected officials as comparatively better [73].",
            "page": 15
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
